course: dictionary
lesson: 2271
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Credit is one of them, so you must use the sentence around it to find the right meaning. Look at the clue words. «A credit card bill shows what was spent that month» tells you credit here is about money you spend and must pay back.
explain: Credit can also mean praise. In «Give credit to others for their ideas», the word give is the clue: it means say thank you and show who had the good idea. If you take credit for work you did not do, you are claiming praise that belongs to someone else.
explain: Always try your meaning in the sentence. «Bell patented the telephone in 1876, though others such as Antonio Meucci worked on similar ideas and credit is argued» means people still disagree about who should be praised for it. If your meaning makes sense, you have probably found the right one.
fact: Credit can mean praise or recognition for something good you have done.
fact: Credit can mean buying something now and paying for it later, which is called buying on credit.
fact: Credit can mean money in an account, on a phone or on a card.
fact: The credits are the list of names at the end of a film or show.
fact: To credit someone means to say they did something good, or to believe them. «Often credited to the Buddha» means people say he said it, though it is probably from later writers.
exercise: «The film ended, and the credits rolled slowly up the screen.» What are the credits? | Money you can spend at the cinema | *The list of names of the people who made the film | Tickets for the next show | A kind of praise for the actors
exercise: Mia says, «Give credit to others for their ideas.» What does credit mean here? | Money in a bank | To believe a rumour | Buying now and paying later | *Praise or recognition
exercise: «A credit score is a rating of how reliably someone pays. Missing payments lowers it.» What do missing payments do to a credit score? | It turns it into a film | It has no effect at all | *It lowers it | It raises it
test: «A credit card bill shows what was spent that month; paying all of it means no interest, paying part makes the debt grow.» Which choice is safer? | *Pay all of the bill | Pay part of the bill and forget the rest | Borrow more on the same card | Pay nothing and wait
test: «Uncle Mo started physiotherapy the same week he bought his magnet bracelet, so the bracelet got the credit for the exercises.» What does got the credit mean? | Was paid for in full | Was thrown away | *Was given the praise, though it was not really the reason | Was cured in one week
exam: Which sentence uses credit to mean praise? | «A credit score is a rating of how reliably someone pays.» | «Radio spread in the 1920s and TV from the 1950s.» | *«Give credit to others for their ideas.» | «Paying part of the bill makes the debt grow.»
